WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.560
HBO's Game of Thrones came to an end in the&nbsp;
spring of 2019. Even its biggest fans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.240
to admit that many story arcs simply don't&nbsp;
add up. These are some of the most puzzling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.120
questions Game of Thrones left in its wake.&nbsp;
Spoilers for the entire series to follow!&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.880
As Game of Thrones begins, King Robert Baratheon&nbsp;
is on the throne of Westeros . Right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.840
viewers learn about his famous conquest of&nbsp;
the Seven Kingdoms with Ned Stark by his side.&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.080
After overthrowing the raving King Aerys Targaryen&nbsp;
, Robert became fiercely protective of his throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:40.000
seeking to kill any young Targaryen threats.
"I’ll kill every Targaryen I get my hands on!"&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.320
In the aftermath of Robert's&nbsp;
death, his illegitimate successor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.840
Joffrey Baratheon takes that&nbsp;
mission several steps forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.840
ordering the systematic killing of Robert's many&nbsp;
bastard children to ensure his own status as king.&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.880
Unfortunately for Joffrey, he misses one important&nbsp;
bastard: Gendry , a gifted young blacksmith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.520
Gendry goes on to survive the entire series, but&nbsp;
his Baratheon blood never proves to be climactic.&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.160
Just before the end of the show, Daenerys&nbsp;
Targaryen legitimizes him, making him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.760
Lord Gendry, despite the fact that doing so&nbsp;could challenge her own claim to the Iron Throne.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.080
One of the most famously infuriating&nbsp;
moments in Thrones' final season is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.040
Daenerys' lightning-fast decision to slaughter&nbsp;thousands of innocents in King's Landing.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.400
As Daenerys and her team prepare to attack King's&nbsp;Landing and Queen Cersei Lannister , Cersei's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.080
brother and Daenerys' right-hand man Tyrion tells&nbsp;the Targaryen heir that when she hears the bells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.920
ring in Westeros' capital city, she must back&nbsp;off. As he explains, the bells mean surrender.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.640
"If the city surrenders, they will&nbsp;
ring the bells and raise the gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.000
Please, if you hear them ringing&nbsp;
the bells, call off the attack."

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.440
This would be all well and good, except that&nbsp;during another crucial battle set at King's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.960
Landing, Varys tells Tyrion that the bells&nbsp;ring for "horror," typically related to war.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.920
Daenerys begins the show as the&nbsp;
khaleesi of a wild Dothraki horde.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.560
After losing and gaining control of&nbsp;
these proud horse lords many times over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.800
she eventually makes her way to Westeros&nbsp;with a massive Dothraki army at her back.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.000
After losing one of her dragons to the Night&nbsp;King, Daenerys finds herself at a disadvantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.280
as the White Walkers head towards Westeros.&nbsp;As the battle begins, seemingly most, if&nbsp;not all of her Dothraki warriors charge,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:40.160
and are&nbsp;immediately snuffed out by the army of the dead. However, just a few episodes later,&nbsp;after Daenerys overtakes King's Landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.320
the camera pans across what looks&nbsp;
like thousands of Dothraki warriors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.120
enthralled by her speech&nbsp;about conquering the world.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.840
David Benioff himself said that the Battle of&nbsp;Winterfell was quote, "essentially the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.160
the Dothraki," so how does Daenerys have so&nbsp;many horse lords left after that frigid battle?

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.560
In the series finale, Tyrion&nbsp;
is given free reign to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.480
the next monarch. He decides&nbsp;
that Bran Stark, who has quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.000
"the best story," should be crowned Bran&nbsp;the Broken, and rule the Seven Kingdoms.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.240
Whether or not Bran actually does&nbsp;
have "the best story," this choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.440
makes little sense. Bran has become the&nbsp;inhuman and omniscient Three-Eyed Raven:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.840
As he has made clear, he cares&nbsp;
about little, including the throne.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.840
After Game of Thrones spent years&nbsp;
signaling that a great leader would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.240
be honorable as well as cunning, why&nbsp;
does his sister Sansa , who is both,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.000
get a junior queenship while&nbsp;
her brother gets the real prize?

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.480
Game of Thrones' biggest mystery&nbsp;
always concerned the White Walkers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.400
an undead race of beings who live&nbsp;
in the far north beyond the Wall.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.040
The White Walkers and their leader, the Night&nbsp;King, pose an enormous threat to all of Westeros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.440
as the army of the dead will eventually make&nbsp;its way to the Seven Kingdoms and wage war.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.920
Throughout the series, viewers get small&nbsp;pieces of information about the Walkers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.960
including that they are given&nbsp;
babies as offerings from humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.400
who live north of the Wall, who&nbsp;
they turn into tiny Walkers.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.960
However, in the season eight episode "The Long&nbsp;Night," when the Walkers finally make their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.760
way to Winterfell, Arya Stark dispatches&nbsp;the Night King without too much trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.800
and the mysteries surrounding the White&nbsp;Walkers die with him and his entire army.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.800
In the seventh season, several of Daenerys'&nbsp;allies go on an ill-advised mission north&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.600
of the Wall to try and prove to Cersei that the&nbsp;White Walkers are real. Southerners like Cersei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:37.200
consider them to be a myth. Thus, Tyrion proposes&nbsp;
capturing a wight and bringing it to show Cersei.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.000
However, this plan is foolish right from&nbsp;
the start. Wights rarely travel alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.520
so trying to capture a single wight and return&nbsp;
it in one piece is essentially a suicide mission.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.880
Also, Cersei is certainly shocked&nbsp;
by the wight when she sees it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.360
but she wages war against them anyway,&nbsp;and all that Daenerys' suicide squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.800
has to show for their trouble is&nbsp;
a stray wight and a felled dragon.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.080
After Daenerys swoops in to rescue&nbsp;
the gang, she suffers a major loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.080
when the Night King shoots one of her&nbsp;
dragons, Viserion, out of the sky.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.480
The image of Viserion's eye&nbsp;
reopening with its new blue hue is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.560
definitely one of Thrones' coolest&nbsp;
visuals, but the mechanics of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.640
the Night King and his army retrieve the dragon&nbsp;
from underneath the ice raises some questions.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:29.120
Earlier in that same episode, the wight-capturers&nbsp;
keep Walkers at bay by isolating themselves on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:35.280
rock surrounded by water, as wights can't swim.&nbsp;
If that's so, how did they retrieve Viserion?

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.640
Beyond that, the army of the dead uses enormous&nbsp;
chains to drag Viserion to the surface:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.800
Where did they get those, and how did&nbsp;
they attach them to the dragon underwater?

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.200
On 'Game of Thrones', one of the most&nbsp;
difficult obstacles is travel. For that reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.280
early seasons of Game of Thrones are&nbsp;
more or less devoted to long journeys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:01.280
making it clear that people like Daenerys, who is&nbsp;
trying to get to Westeros, have a long way to go.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.640
However, as the show continued, writers&nbsp;
focused less on Westeros' sprawling landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.160
to the point where the rules of time and&nbsp;
space seemed to stop mattering entirely.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.200
Journeys that would have once&nbsp;
taken a season and a half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.520
eventually only take a couple of minutes.&nbsp;
Gendry can run from a mountaintop north of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.000
the Wall to send a raven to Daenerys, who&nbsp;
arrives on a dragon shortly thereafter.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.960
Suspension of disbelief is fine for a&nbsp;
show that involves dragons and magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:35.120
but Game of Thrones' space-time continuum develops&nbsp;
not just a tear, but some pretty enormous holes.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.440
Melisandre is a cryptic priestess of R'hllor , the&nbsp;
Lord of Light. Initially, Melisandre, who serves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.200
Stannis Baratheon until his death, is the only red&nbsp;
priest viewers meet, but others eventually appear.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.160
Beyond their fire-related powers, they deliver&nbsp;
prophecies, especially Melisandre, who makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:56.400
sweeping proclamations about&nbsp;
chosen ones and rightful kings.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:03.520
"Stannis Baratheon, warrior of&nbsp;
light, your sword awaits you."

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.280
These prophecies are all exciting in the&nbsp;
beginning, but by the time Game of Thrones draws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.920
to a close, none of them have been fulfilled. When&nbsp;
all is said and done, Melisandre's predictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.400
have little to no bearing on anything in the&nbsp;
show's outcome and are never explained at all.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:23.600
Bronn is a fan-favorite as the&nbsp;
beloved, wise-cracking mercenary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.360
who spends time with both Jaime and&nbsp;
Tyrion Lannister throughout the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.400
But a bizarre plotline takes&nbsp;
place during the eighth season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.600
in which Bronn agrees to kill Jaime and Tyrion&nbsp;
simply because Cersei offered him money.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:40.560
He threatens the brothers with a crossbow, then&nbsp;
isn't seen again until the finale. By then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.640
Tyrion has made him Lord of Highgarden,&nbsp;
which he promised Bronn in exchange for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:48.320
his and Jaime's lives, as well&nbsp;
as Master of Coin to King Bran.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.200
Earlier in the series, Bronn says he's never&nbsp;
borrowed money and doesn't know how it works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.120
so making a man who can't figure out the concept&nbsp;
of a loan the Master of Coin seems unwise.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:03.760
Game of Thrones is home to plenty of&nbsp;
moments that require viewers to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.520
aside some of their logic and reason,&nbsp;
especially when it comes to injuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.360
After characters like Khal Drogo die&nbsp;
of afflictions like blood poisoning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.360
you’d think that a serious injury might&nbsp;
take a character down once in a while.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:21.200
Both Jaime and Arya endure brutal stab wounds to&nbsp;
the stomach during fights: Arya loses a tussle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.760
with the Waif in season six, while Jaime struggles&nbsp;
against Euron Greyjoy in the final season.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.560
And yet, neither person seems much&nbsp;
troubled by these grievous injuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:36.000
Jaime makes it into the Red Keep to find&nbsp;
Cersei, while Arya somehow leaps into a canal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.920
swims across Braavos, and is eventually cured&nbsp;
by a cup of soup and a good night's sleep.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.960
Armor can be effective against&nbsp;
knives, but apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.640
being a series regular can save you&nbsp;
from multiple stomach wounds as well.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:55.360
Beyond her unhelpful prophecies, Melisandre&nbsp;
does a fair amount of magic on Game of Thrones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.360
much of which concerns blood sacrifices.&nbsp;
To help Stannis gain the throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.560
she sacrifices his followers&nbsp;
and countrymen to the flame.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:08.080
She steals "king's blood" from Gendry for a&nbsp;
ritual that will destroy any usurpers to the Iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:14.000
Throne. She even convinces Stannis to burn his&nbsp;
own daughter, Shireen alive, to win the War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:20.640
of the Five Kings. Shireen dies screaming, her&nbsp;
mother hangs herself, and Stannis loses anyway.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.440
With all that in mind, many viewers&nbsp;
were terrified of who or what Melisandre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.840
would sacrifice after she revived a&nbsp;
murdered Jon Snow. This time, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:36.000
Melisandre doesn't have to sacrifice anyone&nbsp;
— she just brings Jon back from the dead.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.320
If she's capable of resurrection&nbsp;
with nothing but her own power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.800
why did she have to kill all those other people?

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.640
After Daenerys’ husband Drogo sustains&nbsp;
an infected wound, the dragon queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.600
can't keep her husband from a grisly&nbsp;
death. Bereaved but newly self-confident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.840
Daenerys sets her sight on Westeros,&nbsp;
putting her romantic life on the backburner.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.000
Though Daenerys takes a few lovers,&nbsp;
she never truly falls in love again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.480
Until Jon Snow arrives, and the two&nbsp;
fall head over heels for each other.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.800
Putting their lackluster chemistry aside —&nbsp;
as well as the fact that they're actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.840
aunt and nephew — Daenerys risking her&nbsp;
quest for the Iron Throne over a lover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.880
is wildly inconsistent with her character.

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.760
It doesn't make sense, until you consider&nbsp;
that, as a plot device, it forces Jon to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:28.960
face some internal conflict about killing the&nbsp;
woman who's&nbsp;not just his queen, but his lover.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.680
Samwell Tarly might be one of Game of Thrones'&nbsp;
most lovable characters, but he's definitely not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:41.200
the toughest fighter. While out in the far North&nbsp;
with a few other men from the Night's Watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.120
Sam and his black-clad brothers&nbsp;
hear a horn sound three times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.280
indicating that White Walkers have been spotted.&nbsp;
While Sam's compatriots make a run for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.400
he can't catch up, and so he hides behind a rock.&nbsp;
This leads to a thrilling scene in which Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.400 --> 00:11:00.080
watches an entire army of Walkers make their way&nbsp;
towards the Wall, but it also raises questions.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:04.880
Not only would he not be able to get ahead of&nbsp;
the army, but a Walker atop an undead horse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.280
makes prolonged eye contact with him.&nbsp;
The White Walkers will kill anything in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:13.840
their path. Besides the fact that he's a&nbsp;
main character, why is Sam any different?

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.680
When audiences first meet Jon Snow and Ned Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.400
they're told that Jon is Ned's shameful bastard,&nbsp;
the product of an affair with a tavern wench.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:28.160
But Ned prizes his honor above all else,&nbsp;
making the situation seem a little shady.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.040
Ultimately, the truth is revealed; Jon isn't a&nbsp;
bastard at all, but is the legitimate product of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:38.640
Ned's sister Lyanna Stark and Daenerys' brother&nbsp;
Prince Rhaegar Targaryen, who married in secret.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:42.000
As Lyanna lay dying with Robert&nbsp;
Baratheon's forces approaching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.640
she begged her brother to keep Jon's real&nbsp;
identity a secret, to save him from Robert's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:52.160
sword. This is true enough — but the lengths to&nbsp;
which Ned goes to keep this secret are extreme.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:56.720
For years, Ned's loving wife Catelyn&nbsp;
resents Jon as shameful proof of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.400
husband's infidelity. Couldn't Ned&nbsp;
have told his wife the real story?

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.440
Jon Snow has one of the toughest&nbsp;
journeys on Game of Thrones, yet somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:10.240
he ends up with a reasonably happy ending.&nbsp;
After helping Daenerys conquer King's Landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:14.800
he realizes that his queen and lover&nbsp;
has turned evil in her quest for power.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.120
In the aftermath of her massacre of King's&nbsp;
Landing's civilians, he stabs her for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.600
good of the realm. Though Daenerys' loyal&nbsp;
soldiers are furious, a deal is reached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:28.560
and Jon is banished back to the Night's&nbsp;
Watch, landing him right back where he began.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:33.200
Jon is always happiest when he's north of&nbsp;
the Wall, so at face value, this seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:38.880
a perfect ending. However, there's a catch: The&nbsp;
Night's Watch no longer has any reason to exist.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.320
The Night's Watchmen protected the Wall&nbsp;
and the realm from White Walkers, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:45.280
without them, there's nothing&nbsp;
for the Night's Watch to do.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.320
It's a neat ending to send Jon&nbsp;
back to his brothers in black,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.840
but it also makes absolutely no sense.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.920
Pretty much everything that Euron&nbsp;
Greyjoy does in the final two seasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:00.720
of Game of Thrones makes no sense. As&nbsp;
Thrones' final conflict draws near,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.120
Euron's niece and nephew, Yara&nbsp;
and Theon , steal his best ships.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:09.360
Euron manages to build 1,000 ships&nbsp;
in pretty quick order and from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:15.120
he gains perfect aim overnight, taking down one&nbsp;
of Daenerys' typically impervious dragons with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.560
perfect shot — from a moving ship, no less.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:24.720
He's at multiple major disadvantages, yet none&nbsp;
of them seem to actually matter. In a show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:30.640
prides itself on taking fantasy tropes seriously,&nbsp;
Euron Greyjoy stands out like a lucky sore thumb.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:37.120
Out of all the characters on Game of Thrones,&nbsp;
Tywin Lannister is probably the smartest man in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:42.560
all of Westeros. Tywin is a political master&nbsp;
who stops at nothing in his quest to elevate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:48.160
Lannisters. This makes one of his personal&nbsp;
relationships extraordinarily confusing.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.920
During the show's second season, while Arya&nbsp;
is on the run from King's Landing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:56.000
wake of her father's execution, she ends&nbsp;
up being Tywin's cupbearer at Harrenhal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.640
The two strike up a solid rapport, especially&nbsp;
when Tywin realizes that she's well-educated.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:05.600
However, that fact alone should have revealed&nbsp;
Arya's identity. Tywin knows that one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:11.760
two Stark girls has escaped, and Arya's knowledge&nbsp;
betrays her highborn status almost immediately.

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:16.000
Tywin may be the cleverest man&nbsp;
in Westeros, but apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.640
even his considerable brain has its limits.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:23.040
It takes Daenerys until the start of&nbsp;
Thrones' seventh season to arrive in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:27.520
Westeros. She makes a triumphant return&nbsp;
to Dragonstone, her ancestral home.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:31.520
In a near-silent scene that closes&nbsp;
the seventh season's premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:37.040
Daenerys strides across a shore she barely&nbsp;
remembers. At last, she's finally ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:43.520
take on her Westerosi foes. However, there's&nbsp;
one thing about this scene that doesn't add up.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:48.400
After Stannis' death, why didn't anyone&nbsp;
acquainted with the Lannister forces even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:53.680
bother trying to take Dragonstone? As a coastal&nbsp;
stronghold, it would have been wise for Daenerys'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:58.240
enemies to send even just a few soldiers&nbsp;
to protect the place where she was born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.200
especially because they&nbsp;
likely knew she would return.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:06.560
Cersei might be a vicious power player&nbsp;
throughout Thrones, but she does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:10.480
truly love her children. Watching&nbsp;
her lose her two younger children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:14.320
in incredibly tragic ways almost&nbsp;
makes you pity the villainess.

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:24.320
"She was good. From her first breath, she was&nbsp;
so sweet. I don’t know where she came from."

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:30.160
However, we know long before her last child&nbsp;
dies that losing children was her destiny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:34.640
In the fifth season's premiere, a young Cersei&nbsp;
meets a witch who tells her that she will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:38.480
three children, and that their crowns&nbsp;
and shrouds will both be golden.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:43.520
Yet in the show's second episode, Cersei tells&nbsp;
Catelyn Stark that she once had a brown-haired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:47.520
baby, presumably the baby she had with&nbsp;
Robert Baratheon. That would mean she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:53.920
had four children, making this otherwise&nbsp;
correct prophecy suddenly null and void.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:59.040
In the series' final episode, Bran is chosen&nbsp;
as the next King of the Seven Kingdoms, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:04.320
before anyone can celebrate, Sansa asks that the&nbsp;
North, which has always belonged to the Starks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:09.600
maintain its independence. Bran agrees,&nbsp;
and Sansa is crowned Queen in the North.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:14.240
It's pretty strange that neither Yara Greyjoy,&nbsp;
who has been angling for the Iron Islands'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:18.800
independence, nor the unnamed prince of Dorne,&nbsp;
a region with a long history of independence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:25.120
from the other kingdoms, asks for the same thing.
Sansa gets what she wants — so why don't they ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:30.480
too, instead of accepting the fact that their&nbsp;
precious kingdoms remain under Bran's rule?&nbsp;

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:36.080
During Thrones' fifth and sixth seasons,&nbsp;
Arya undergoes a massive transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:40.800
when she trains at the House of the Undying in&nbsp;
Braavos . It is here where she learns to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:46.560
faceless assassin, and the mysterious girl&nbsp;
known as the Waif plays a huge role in it.&nbsp;

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:51.120
As the apparent right-hand woman to&nbsp;
Jaqen H'ghar, the Waif despises Arya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:56.880
beating her without mercy after Arya loses her&nbsp;
sight, and eventually trying to assassinate her.&nbsp;

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:01.040
Aside from setting up conflict for Arya,&nbsp;
however, there's not really much reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:06.560
for the Waif to exist, and her presence and&nbsp;
animosity towards Arya, go completely unexplained.&nbsp;

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:12.080
Once Arya leaves Braavos, we never see either&nbsp;
Jaqen or the Waif again, and it's even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:18.080
unclear as to why the Waif ever existed.
After learning to assume other people's faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:23.120
and kill her enemies in Braavos, Arya proves&nbsp;
herself to be a formidable assassin, taking down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:28.880
her enemies as soon as she arrives in Westeros.
For years, however, one enemy looms above all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:34.320
the rest: Cersei Lannister. It seems like a&nbsp;
foregone conclusion for much of the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:40.240
that Arya will be the one to kill Cersei.
However, Arya does not kill Cersei. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:44.400
the Hound warns her that killing Cersei&nbsp;
will end up killing her as well, as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:49.600
Red Keep is on the verge of collapse.
Somehow, that one sentence works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:54.800
and Arya turns back, and Cersei's death&nbsp;
fails to bring Arya's story to a close.

